minneapolis is bracing for the  high-profile trial of derrick chauvin  the former police officer charged with  second-degree murder and second-degree  manslaughter  in the death of george floyd  i cannot breathe  video of the incident in which chauvin  is seen kneeling on floyd's neck while  floyd repeated that he couldn't breathe  sparked worldwide protests and launched  a national reckoning over police  brutality against black people in the  united states  the trial will be live streamed and  authorities expect it to inflame  tensions once again more than 3 000 law  enforcement officers and national guard  troops could be on call as the jury  deliberates  this is a difficult case in so many ways  people around the world have seen this  footage but  people in minneapolis have seen the  footage and what it's done to the city i  mean you  had riots a billion dollars worth of  damage to  businesses 1500 businesses were you know  somehow impacted by this  we recognize that throughout the trial  itself people will want to gather  they'll want to protest  they'll want to express their first  amendment rights and we want to enable  them to do so  safely and peacefully due to concerns  surrounding covet 19 and social  distancing  chauvin will be tried alone his lawyer  has made filings indicating he's not  guilty  so what kind of evidence will be  presented who might testify  and what is the bar for conviction here  are three things to know about chauvin's  trial  first video evidence is expected to play  a significant role  the prosecution is likely to seize on  the footage that shows chauvin kneeling  on floyd's neck  for around eight minutes as prosecutors  have pointed out previously  chauvin continued to kneel on floyd's  neck for about four minutes after an  officer said floyd was passing out  and for two and a half minutes after  another officer said floyd did not have  a pulse  relax i think  those last minutes after he's  already no longer not only not moving  but um  they can't even find a pulse how the  jury is going to react to  you know seeing that again in court as  probably one of the big moments of the  trial  the defense may also use video evidence  to bolster its case  though it is likely to focus on what  happened before those final minutes  you got him dang man put your hands on  top of your head  [Music]  chauvin's attorney has previously argued  that police body camera footage  shows floyd's struggling and actively  resisting arrest  stop resisting man i'm not yes you are  the defense has also pointed to footage  of floyd saying he couldn't breathe  before he was ever on the ground as  evidence that serious medical issues  were the cause of his death  medical evidence will also feature  prominently in the trial  but the jury may hear conflicting  testimony especially because two  different autopsies determined that  floyd's death was a homicide  but gave different reasons for his death  the defense  only wants to have the official medical  examiner's report  which talks about george floyd dying  because of  cardiac arrest complicated by the police  trying to arrest him and subdue him  and then also mentioning that he had  fentanyl and  methamphetamines and a heart condition  chauvin's attorney has pointed to that  autopsy to argue that floyd couldn't  breathe  due to a lethal dose of fentanyl and  possibly a speedball  and that other pre-existing conditions  combined with the drugs  most likely led to his death but another  autopsy prepared for floyd's family came  to a different conclusion that he died  of asphyxiation at the hands of the  police  well i think the question the two sides  are going to be going back and forth on  is what standard procedure and what is  police training so what is the bar for a  conviction  the most serious charge chauvin is  facing is second-degree unintentional  murder  that means prosecutors don't need to  prove intent to kill  but they must prove beyond a reasonable  doubt that chauvin intentionally  inflicted substantial bodily harm to  floyd which resulted in floyd's death  what they are actually trying to prove  is that  in the course of him being assaulted by  the police  he died so the police didn't intend to  kill him but by assaulting him  it led to his death chauvin's attorney  argued that the officers properly  restrained floyd because he was acting  erratically and posed a risk of injury  to himself  and potentially to the officers it  really is a difficult  thing to come to grips with because  everybody's seen this video  the officers were all fired immediately  but it's still very difficult to  prosecute  a police officer in this country legally  the stakes are high  if chauvin is acquitted the three other  officers may not go to trial at all  for aiding and abetting him currently  that trial has been delayed until august  lawyers for the three have made filings  indicating they aren't  guilty
 

